T20.01|Debt securities promise specified payments rather than residual ownership cash. Government bonds, treasury bills and corporate bonds differ in issuer, tenor, payment structure and risk. Sovereign status does not eliminate currency, inflation or default exposure in every setting.~~A bond's contract determines coupons, principal, priority and optionality. Quoted yield is not a guaranteed realised return if the investor sells early, faces default or reinvests coupons at different rates.|Government security: Claim on a public issuer.;;Treasury bill: Typically short-dated discount security under market rules.;;Corporate bond: Debt claim on a business issuer.|Buy a zero-coupon instrument for ₹95 that promises ₹100 in one year. Assume timely full payment.~~Gain is ₹5.;;One-year return is 5/95 ≈ 5.26%.;;If recovery is only ₹90, realised return is −5.26%.~~A promised yield depends on payment and purchase price.~~Inflation and currency can also change purchasing-power results.|Read issuer and payment terms.;;Identify default, rate and currency risks.;;Distinguish promised yield from realised return.
T20.02|Municipal and agency debt involve specified public or related issuers; covered bonds add defined collateral support; inflation-linked bonds adjust eligible cash flows under an indexation rule. Guarantees, legal recourse and tax treatment differ by instrument and jurisdiction.~~Collateral or indexation reduces certain risks while leaving others. Inflation protection against one official index may not match an investor's personal expenses, and indexation can have lags or floors.|Municipal or agency claim: Particular public-entity or agency exposure.;;Covered bond: Issuer claim with specified asset-pool protection.;;Inflation-linked bond: Eligible amounts follow an index rule.|Assume a bond's ₹100 principal is indexed by a 4% price-index increase and its 2% coupon applies to adjusted principal.~~Adjusted principal is ₹104.;;Coupon is 2% × ₹104 = ₹2.08.;;Review actual index dates, floors and payment conventions.~~Indexation changes both principal and the coupon base in this assumed structure.~~Not every linked bond follows these exact terms.|Read guarantee and recourse details.;;Check indexation conventions.;;Assess remaining credit, liquidity and real-rate exposure.
T20.03|Coupon structure specifies how interest payments are determined. Fixed coupons use a stated rate; floating coupons reset from a benchmark plus spread; zero-coupon bonds pay no periodic interest and compensate through price and maturity value. Payment frequency and day-count rules matter.~~Coupon rate is not investment yield unless special conditions hold. Price, time, default and options determine the return implied by the security.|Fixed coupon: Predetermined rate or schedule.;;Floating coupon: Benchmark-linked resets.;;Zero coupon: No interim coupon payment.|A ₹100 face bond has 6% annual coupon paid semiannually under a simple equal-period convention.~~Each half-year payment is ₹3.;;Annual coupon totals ₹6.;;If purchased for ₹90, current yield is 6/90 ≈ 6.67%, not 6%.~~Coupon and yield refer to different bases.~~Actual accrual and settlement conventions can alter period calculations.|Read rate, frequency and day count.;;Separate face value from market price.;;Model all contractual cash flows.
T20.04|Clean price excludes accrued interest; dirty or full price includes it and usually represents the basic settlement amount before fees. Accrued interest allocates the coupon between seller and buyer according to the market's convention.~~A clean-price chart can differ from total return because coupons and accrual are omitted. Day-count rules and ex-coupon treatment are market-specific.|Clean price: Quote excluding eligible accrued interest.;;Accrued interest: Coupon portion assigned to elapsed accrual.;;Dirty price: Clean plus accrued interest under the stated convention.|Clean price is ₹98 and accrued interest ₹2 per ₹100 face.~~Dirty price is ₹100.;;Buying ₹10,000 face costs ₹10,000 before fees under these quotes.;;Compare full prices and coupons when calculating holding-period return.~~A ₹98 screen quote does not necessarily mean a ₹98 settlement payment.~~Verify units and the actual market convention.|Identify quotation per face unit.;;Use the correct accrual basis.;;Reconcile settlement and total return.
T20.05|Bond pricing discounts each expected contractual payment using appropriate factors, with credit and option assumptions where relevant. For an option-free fixed-coupon bond, higher required yields generally mean lower prices. A flat-yield formula is a simplification of a full term-structure approach.~~Promised cash flows should not be treated as certain when default is material. Optional redemption or conversion can change both cash-flow timing and valuation.|Discount bond: Price below face under the specified cash flows.;;Par bond: Price equals face.;;Premium bond: Price above face.|A one-year bond pays ₹10 coupon and ₹100 principal. Required one-year yield is 20%, assuming full payment.~~Final receipt is ₹110.;;Price = ₹110/1.20 = ₹91.67.;;At 10% required yield, price is ₹100.~~The same promised payment has different value at different required yields.~~Real bonds may require curve-specific discounting and credit modelling.|List dates and amounts.;;Use coherent yield or discount factors.;;Include credit and embedded-option effects.
T20.06|Current yield is annual coupon divided by price; YTM is the rate equating price with promised payments to maturity under its convention. YTC uses a specified call date and redemption amount; yield to worst considers relevant contractual redemption outcomes under the methodology.~~YTM is not automatically the realised compound return: default, sale timing and coupon reinvestment can change results. Compare yields only after matching compounding and cash-flow conventions.|Current yield: Coupon/price.;;YTM: Promised cash-flow rate to maturity.;;YTC or yield to worst: Contractual early-redemption analysis.|A bond bought for ₹100 pays ₹5 coupon and may be called in one year for ₹101. Assume that call occurs and no other flows.~~Current yield is 5%.;;One-year return to this call is (₹5 + ₹101)/₹100 − 1 = 6%.;;Its maturity yield needs the separate maturity schedule.~~Different yield labels use different payment horizons.~~The issuer need not choose the investor's preferred outcome.|Read every yield convention.;;Model call dates and prices.;;Assess reinvestment and default separately.
T20.07|Spot rates price single maturity-specific cash flows; forward rates are rates implied between future dates by current discount factors; par yields make a specified coupon bond price at face. They are related but answer different questions.~~An implied forward is a no-arbitrage pricing relation under stated assumptions, not a guarantee of the future realised short rate. Risk premiums and market expectations both influence the curve.|Spot rate: Discount rate to one maturity.;;Forward rate: Implied rate between dates.;;Par yield: Coupon rate consistent with par price.|One-year spot is 5% and two-year annual-compound spot is 6%.~~Two-year growth factor is 1.06².;;Implied year-two forward factor is 1.06²/1.05.;;Forward rate is about 7.01%.~~A two-year 6% spot rate is not the same as the second year's forward rate.~~This relation assumes compatible instruments, conventions and funding conditions.|Use consistent compounding.;;Derive from discount factors.;;Separate implied pricing from future-rate prediction.
T20.08|A yield curve relates rates to maturities under a defined instrument and credit basis. Upward, flat, inverted or hump-shaped curves can reflect expected future rates, risk premiums, liquidity and supply-demand conditions. One explanation rarely identifies all causes.~~A government curve and a corporate curve include different risks. Changes can be parallel, steepening, flattening or curved, so one duration number may miss maturity-specific exposure.|Expectations view: Future-rate beliefs contribute to yields.;;Term-premium view: Compensation for maturity risk matters.;;Market-segmentation view: Investor demand and supply affect maturities.|One-year yield is 7% and ten-year yield 5%.~~The selected curve is inverted.;;Consider expectations of lower future rates and changing term premiums.;;Test a portfolio under nonparallel movements rather than assuming every tenor falls together.~~An inversion is an observed shape, not a guaranteed recession or trading outcome.~~Credit and liquidity differences can distort comparisons.|Specify curve instruments and date.;;Analyse several maturity changes.;;Distinguish observed shape from causal interpretation.
T20.09|Bootstrapping derives maturity-specific discount factors sequentially from instrument prices and known earlier factors. A coupon bond contains several payments, so its price cannot be assigned directly to one maturity without removing the already-valued earlier payments.~~Reliable bootstrapping needs consistent instruments, settlement conventions and interpolation. Credit or liquidity differences can produce an inconsistent curve if mixed carelessly.|Short-maturity factor: Established from an eligible short instrument.;;Sequential factor: Solve remaining maturity after earlier flows.;;Interpolation: Estimate between observed maturities under a stated method.|Assume one-year discount factor 0.95. A two-year bond with ₹5 annual coupon and ₹100 principal costs ₹98.~~First-coupon PV = ₹5 × 0.95 = ₹4.75.;;Remaining PV is ₹98 − ₹4.75 = ₹93.25.;;Two-year factor = ₹93.25/₹105 ≈ 0.8881.~~The final factor follows after separating the earlier coupon.~~Actual yield conversion depends on the chosen compounding convention.|Use comparable instruments.;;Reconcile each price to cash-flow PVs.;;Document interpolation and settlement choices.
T20.10|Macaulay duration is the present-value-weighted average time to cash receipt. Modified duration approximates price sensitivity to a yield change under specified conventions. Effective duration reprices the instrument under curve shifts and can accommodate cash-flow changes from options.~~Duration is a local approximation, not a guaranteed percentage loss. Large shifts, changing spreads and embedded options require additional analysis.|Macaulay duration: Weighted timing measure.;;Modified duration: Yield-based price sensitivity.;;Effective duration: Repricing-based sensitivity.|A bond has modified duration 5 and full price ₹100. Assume a small 1-percentage-point yield increase and ignore convexity.~~Approximate percentage change = −5 × 0.01 = −5%.;;Estimated new price is ₹95.;;For a larger move or callable bond, use fuller repricing and suitable risk measures.~~Rate units must be decimals in the formula.~~Credit spread and currency effects are separate exposures.|Select the correct duration type.;;State yield and curve conventions.;;Assess approximation limits and other risks.
T20.11|Convexity captures curvature in the price-yield relationship, improving the linear duration approximation for suitable bonds. Key-rate duration estimates exposure to changes around particular curve maturities rather than assuming one parallel move.~~Callable and other option-embedded instruments can show different curvature, including negative convexity in relevant regions. Numerical measures depend on the repricing model and shock size.|Convexity: Second-order price response.;;Key-rate duration: Tenor-specific curve exposure.;;Parallel versus nonparallel shock: Whole-curve shift or shape change.|Assume modified duration 5, convexity 30 and yield increase 0.01.~~Duration effect is −5%.;;Convexity adjustment is 0.5 × 30 × 0.01² = 0.15%.;;Combined approximate change is −4.85%.~~Curvature moderates the estimated loss in this positive-convexity case.~~Definitions and units must match; this formula is not universal for option-dependent cash flows.|Use compatible convexity units.;;Test curve shape changes.;;Reprice nonlinear or large exposures directly.
T20.12|A credit spread compares a risky bond with a benchmark under a defined methodology. It reflects default-related, liquidity and other pricing influences. Spread duration estimates price sensitivity to spread changes, while benchmark-rate duration addresses another risk source.~~A spread increase can reduce price even if government yields do not move. Different measures such as nominal, zero-volatility or option-adjusted spreads have different assumptions.|Nominal spread: Yield difference against a benchmark.;;Curve-based or option-adjusted spread: Model-based comparison.;;Spread duration: Sensitivity to spread movement.|A bond has spread duration 4. Assume its spread widens 50 basis points with other conditions fixed.~~Spread change is 0.005.;;Approximate price change is −4 × 0.005 = −2%.;;Separately model any benchmark-rate movement.~~Interest-rate and spread risk can move independently or together.~~Default events and liquidity gaps can exceed the local approximation.|Identify the spread measure.;;Separate benchmark and credit effects.;;Stress default, liquidity and spread shifts.
T20.13|Floating-rate notes reset coupons from a benchmark plus contractual spread. Frequent resets can reduce sensitivity to benchmark-rate changes near par, but do not eliminate credit, liquidity, basis or contractual risk. Floors, caps and reset lags alter behaviour.~~A fixed coupon spread can become unattractive if the issuer's required credit spread widens. The price may then fall even though the benchmark portion resets.|Benchmark component: Variable reference rate.;;Contractual margin: Fixed or defined added spread.;;Reset and optional features: Dates, floors and caps.|A ₹100 face note pays benchmark 5% plus 2%. At the next reset, benchmark becomes 6%.~~Coupon rate changes from 7% to 8%, assuming no floor or cap.;;Annualised payment changes from ₹7 to ₹8.;;If credit quality worsens, market price can still decline despite the reset.~~Floating rates reduce one exposure while retaining others.~~Actual payment uses period and day-count terms.|Read benchmark and reset rules.;;Assess margin versus current credit risk.;;Model caps, floors and funding basis.
T20.14|Callable bonds give the issuer specified redemption rights; puttable bonds give investors specified exit rights; convertible bonds add equity-conversion features. Optionality changes expected timing, price sensitivity and claim value.~~A high coupon on a callable bond may be compensation for reinvestment and call risk. Conversion exposure means a bond's value can depend substantially on equity outcomes as well as credit and rates.|Issuer call: Issuer can redeem under terms.;;Investor put: Holder can require specified repayment.;;Conversion: Claim can become equity under a ratio.|A ₹100 face bond pays 6% and can be called at ₹100 when equivalent new borrowing costs 3%.~~The issuer may have an incentive to refinance, subject to call dates and costs.;;The investor may lose future 6% payments through redemption.;;Analyse yield to relevant calls rather than assuming coupons continue to maturity.~~Issuer flexibility can limit investor upside when rates fall.~~Actual exercise is not certain and depends on terms.|Read option dates and prices.;;Model alternative cash-flow paths.;;Assess rate, credit and equity interactions.
T20.15|Default interrupts promised payment; recovery depends on asset proceeds, legal priority, costs and timing. Covenants constrain specified borrower actions or require tests, but do not guarantee repayment. Secured, senior and subordinated claims can suffer different losses.~~A rating or collateral label should not replace recovery analysis. Assets can fall in value during distress, and lengthy enforcement lowers present value.|Default probability: Chance of a defined failure.;;Recovery and LGD: Proceeds and loss conditional on failure.;;Covenant protection: Contractual restrictions and remedies.|A ₹100 lakh bond claim receives ₹40 net recovery after default, assumed immediate.~~Loss is ₹60.;;Recovery rate is 40%; LGD is 60%.;;Delayed recovery would have lower present value at a positive discount rate.~~Loss severity matters separately from default frequency.~~The legal waterfall and actual costs require case-specific evidence.|Read priority and security.;;Stress recovery and timing.;;Track covenant headroom and enforcement limits.
T20.16|Bond ladders spread maturities; immunisation seeks to match defined liability exposures; total-return strategies manage income and price risk against a mandate. Duration matching can reduce sensitivity to small rate changes but requires further assumptions, monitoring and rebalancing.~~Cash-flow matching can provide closer liability alignment when suitable instruments exist. Credit events, nonparallel moves and reinvestment can undermine a simple duration match.|Ladder: Multiple scheduled maturities.;;Immunisation: Match specified value and rate exposures.;;Cash-flow matching: Align receipts with liability dates and amounts.|A liability requires ₹100 lakh in three years. Assume a default-free instrument in the same currency pays exactly ₹100 at that date.~~The contractual receipt matches the dated liability.;;Avoid counting its purchase cash as a future extra receipt.;;Assess whether fees, inflation or settlement timing introduce a mismatch.~~Matching cash dates differs from merely buying a fund with three-year average maturity.~~Real instruments and liabilities may not be perfectly matchable.|Define liability dates and amounts.;;Assess credit and currency consistency.;;Monitor curve and cash-flow mismatches.
T20.17|Fixed-income attribution explains performance through carry, curve movements, spread changes, currency and selection under a stated method. Relative value compares pricing of economically related instruments after adjusting for risks and costs.~~A cheap-looking spread can compensate for weaker liquidity, options or collateral rather than imply mispricing. Attribution conventions allocate interactions differently and should reconcile to total return.|Carry and roll: Income and passage-of-time effects.;;Curve and spread: Benchmark and credit-pricing changes.;;Currency or selection: Other defined contributors.|Buy a bond for ₹100, receive ₹3 coupon and sell for ₹102, with no costs or reinvestment.~~Holding-period return = (₹102 + ₹3 − ₹100)/₹100 = 5%.;;Income contributes 3 percentage points and price change 2 under this simple decomposition.;;Analyse curve and spread drivers within the ₹2 price change.~~Total return includes more than coupon yield.~~Fees, accrued interest and currencies require consistent treatment.|Reconcile total return first.;;State attribution conventions.;;Compare relative value after matching risks and costs.
